      sources: "Sources: Learn, alerts with Azure Policy; Monitor App Service (alert types); az monitor activity-log alert. Chapter 01, “Lab 2: a test subscription.”",
      say: `Lab 2 moves to an Azure test subscription in a test tenant, never production, and an empty list of events is a valid result. Grant the reader its two roles, sign in in the right cloud, and cross-check: Learn's own query and the kit's collection should agree. Then plan the alert policy at the test subscription, have a second person approve the hash, apply it, and check. Learn says the remediation creates a resource group, an alert rule and an action group that emails subscription owners; Service Health alerts are activity log alerts. Finally, back it all out.`,
      detail: `Use an Azure test subscription in a test tenant, never production; it may have no Service Health events, which is a valid result. Placeholders: <subscription-id>, <reader-object-id>, <settings-file> (your copy of deploy/settings.template.json with environment test, only azure enabled, the subscription in providers.azure.subscriptions, a test outputRoot, schedule.taskName empty, and azurePolicy enabled with scope /subscriptions/<subscription-id> and a location), <plan-sha256>, <rollback-sha256>. 1 Grant the reader its roles, as a subscription Owner: az role assignment create --role "Reader" and --role "Service Health Security Reader" at /subscriptions/<subscription-id>. 2 Sign in: az cloud set --name AzureCloud; az login; az extension add --name resource-graph. 3 Cross-check with Learn's own query (summarize count() by eventType), then ./Invoke-PnCollect.ps1 -SettingsPath <settings-file> -Provider azure -OutputPath out/azure-test and group the records by kind. The two counts should agree, once the kit's merge of one event across subscriptions is taken into account. If a security advisory's summary is empty, the reader lacks Service Health Security Reader. 4 Plan the alert policy at the test subscription, signed in as a person who can create policy and role assignments there: az policy definition show --name 98903777-... to read the real role, then New-PnAlertPolicy.ps1; a second person approves the plan's hash. 5 Apply with -Apply -ApprovedSha256 <plan-sha256>; az policy remediation show --name pn-service-health-alerts-remediation; az monitor activity-log alert list --resource-group rg-serviceHealthAlert. Learn says the remediation creates rg-serviceHealthAlert, the alert rule ServiceHealthSubscriptionAlertRule and the action group ag-ServiceHealthAlertActionGroup, emailing subscription owners; Service Health alerts are activity log alerts. 6 Back it out: Invoke-PnRollback.ps1 to plan, then apply with <rollback-sha256>; az group delete --name rg-serviceHealthAlert --yes; remove both role assignments; delete the output folders. Deleting the assignment doesn't delete the resources its remediation made, which is why the resource group is deleted separately here; in production the change record decides.`,
