      say: `Each output is evidence for something specific, against NIST's control catalog. The reader's roles, and the fact that nothing can write, support monitoring an external service provider. The run folder shows every event in scope was read, which supports receiving security alerts and advisories. Valid records and unchanged fingerprints support continuous monitoring. The approved alert plan supports the same, and an approved rollback is configuration change control and contingency planning. The key security indicators are FedRAMP's, and which output supports which indicator is the program's mapping. The kit's records support these controls; the agency's assessor decides them.`,
      detail: `The collection and the alert policy are what the guide's G1 checks. The mappings are the kit's config/requirements.json, which the guide verified against NIST's OSCAL catalog and FedRAMP's KSI pages. The reader holds Reader, and Service Health Security Reader for security advisories, and nothing that writes (Lab 1 step 1; Lab 2 step 1): SA-9 c (monitor the provider's control compliance); KSI-SCR-MIT, KSI-SCR-MON; the cloud platform lead, the ISSO keeps the role assignments. Every Service Health event for the subscriptions in scope was read, with the calls and their roles recorded (raw/azure.json, manifest.json): SA-9 c; SI-5 a (receive advisories); KSI-SCR-MIT, KSI-SCR-MON; the ISSO, with the run folder. Each notice is one record, valid against the schema (notices.json, notices.csv): SI-5; CA-7 (continuous monitoring); KSI-SCR-MON, KSI-MLA-EVC; the ISSO. A re-read changes no fingerprint, so a notice is routed once: CA-7; KSI-MLA-EVC; the repository's tests. The alert path between collections is planned, approved by its hash and applied (plan.json, results.json, the change record): SI-5; CA-7; KSI-SCR-MON, KSI-MLA-EVC; the cloud platform lead, the change record holds the approved hash. The policy assignment was removed by an approved rollback: CM-3 (configuration change control); CP-2; KSI-CMT-LMC, KSI-RPL-ARP; the change record. KSIs are FedRAMP 20x indicators for cloud service providers; which output supports which indicator is the program's mapping (design guidance). The agency authorizes its own system and lines this evidence up with what its providers report against KSIs; it doesn't report KSIs itself. The kit's records support these controls; the agency's assessor decides them.`,
